{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you tonight. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:32:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been vomiting nonstop since yesterday. I can’t keep anything down — not even water. It feels just like my last gastroparesis flare."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You have Type 1 diabetes and a history of gastroparesis, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, and I have a gastric pacer, so MRIs are out. My PCP said I shouldn’t have one, which was part of the plan before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:35:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important — we’ll keep that in mind. You’re not having abdominal pain, fever, or dizziness when standing?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No pain, no fever. I feel weak, but I’m not dizzy when I stand. I did pass gas and had a bowel movement yesterday, though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:37:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s reassuring — it means your bowels are moving, so we don’t suspect a blockage. We’ll start IV fluids and some anti-nausea meds right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2198-03-30 03:38:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thank you. I just need to stop vomiting. I’m so dehydrated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve given you Zofran, Compazine, Reglan, and Ativan through the IV. How are you feeling now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:16:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little better, but still nauseous. I haven’t thrown up since I got here, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:17:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. Your blood pressure was high in the ER — 181 over 93 — likely from vomiting and dehydration. We gave a dose of IV metoprolol to bring it down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on lisinopril and metoprolol at home. Should I keep taking them?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:20:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, once you can tolerate pills, we’ll restart your oral metoprolol. Lisinopril too, but we’ll watch your kidney function."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:21:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. My blood sugar’s probably high too, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:22:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your glucose was 327 on admission, and your urine showed ketones. We’re not sure if it’s full DKA, but we’re treating it like mild ketoacidosis with insulin and fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been using my glargine at night and lispro as needed. Should I adjust anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2198-03-30 04:25:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re continuing your usual insulin regimen — no changes. We’ll monitor your sugars closely and adjust the sliding scale as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2198-03-30 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your nausea seems a bit better? We’re going to try a small sip of water to see if you can tolerate it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2198-03-30 08:01:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel a little less nauseous. I’ll try the water."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2198-03-30 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great — you kept the water down. We’ll advance to ice chips and maybe clear liquids later today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2198-03-30 08:16:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I was worried I’d never be able to eat again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2198-03-30 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs from this morning show your kidney function is improving — creatinine down to 0.6. Your glucose is now 152, and ketones are clearing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2198-03-30 12:31:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. What about the tachycardia? My heart’s been racing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2198-03-30 12:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your heart rate was 133 in the ER — likely from dehydration and nausea. The EKG shows sinus tachycardia, no acute changes. It’s down to 95 now, so it’s improving with fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2198-03-30 12:34:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad it’s not something serious with my heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2198-03-30 12:35:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Me too. The EKG had some non-specific changes, but nothing new compared to your last one. Likely due to lead placement or body habitus."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2198-03-30 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve been on clear liquids all afternoon and haven’t vomited. We’re going to try a soft diet — applesauce and crackers — in small amounts."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2198-03-30 16:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m nervous, but I’m hungry. I’ll try it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2198-03-30 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Excellent — you kept the soft food down. Your nausea is much improved. We’ll keep you on metoclopramide three times a day and add ondansetron as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2198-03-30 18:31:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I keep using the Compazine suppository at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2198-03-30 18:32:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll send it home with you for breakthrough nausea. Also, continue pantoprazole twice daily — it helps with reflux, which can worsen gastroparesis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2198-03-30 20:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been thinking — my last gastric emptying study was normal. Does that mean it’s not really gastroparesis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2198-03-30 20:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good question. You did have a normal study recently, but you’ve had abnormal ones in the past. Symptoms can flare even if tests are normal now. We’re treating based on your clinical picture."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2198-03-30 20:03:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. I just want to avoid another hospital stay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2198-03-31 06:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Your labs look great — glucose is 134, no ketones, and your electrolytes are stable. Hemoglobin dropped a bit, but it’s likely from dilution with fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2198-03-31 06:01:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is the urine test okay? I heard they did a culture."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2198-03-31 06:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the culture showed mixed bacteria, but it’s consistent with contamination — not a UTI. So no antibiotics needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2198-03-31 06:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to know. So what’s next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done really well. You’re tolerating food, nausea is gone, and your vitals are stable. We’re going to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great news. What should I do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue all your usual medications — insulin, metoprolol, lisinopril, metoclopramide, and pantoprazole. Use ondansetron or Compazine if nausea returns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Follow up with your PCP and endocrinologist. If nausea returns, call them early — don’t wait until you’re dehydrated again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:06:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do that. Thank you for helping me feel better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2198-03-31 14:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we hope not to see you back here soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2198-03-30 03:31:00",
    "admission_end": "2198-03-31 15:27:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 30-year-old woman with Type 1 diabetes and a history of gastroparesis was admitted for intractable nausea and vomiting, unable to tolerate oral intake. She was managed with NPO status, IV fluids, and antiemetics including ondansetron, prochlorperazine, metoclopramide, and lorazepam. Her elevated blood glucose and ketonuria suggested mild diabetic ketoacidosis, which resolved with insulin and hydration. Hypertension and tachycardia, likely secondary to dehydration and vomiting, improved with IV fluids and resumed oral antihypertensives. She gradually advanced to a soft diet, tolerated oral intake, and returned to her baseline. A urine culture showed contamination, not infection. She was discharged home with instructions to continue her regular medications and follow up with her primary care provider and endocrinologist.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Diabetes with ketoacidosis, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Polyneuropathy in diabetes",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Reflux esophagitis",
      "Dehydration",
      "Tachycardia, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
